<li><a href="#formatting"> Foramtting text</a>
</ul>

<p><div class=sectiontitle><a name="file">Writing the log to a text file</a></div>

<p>The command <pre>logger::open_file("log.txt");</pre> opens the
named file and the log will be written also to this file. The file is
closed by <pre>logger::close_file();</pre> </p>

<p>In order to automatically limit the scope of the log file, without
the need to take care at the end of a block, or function, use the
utility class <pre>logger::file</pre>:

<pre>
logger::file logfile("log.txt");
</pre>

The constructor of this file launches
<pre>logger::open_file("log.txt")</pre>, and its destructor calls
<pre>logger::close_file();</pre> automatically (whether due to its
scope expiring, or an exception being thrown, etc)
</p>


<p><div class=sectiontitle><a name="html">Writing the log to a html file</a></div>

<p>The command <pre>logger::open_html_file("log.html");</pre> opens the
named file and the log will be written also to this html file with
interactivity and pretty formatting. The file is
closed by <pre>logger::close_html_file();</pre>. Calling the open
function multiple times automatically closes the previous
file. Indented blocks are collapsed by default in the html file, and
can be interactively expanded</p>

<p>In order to automatically limit the scope of the log file, without
the need to take care at the end of a block, or function, use the
utility class <pre>logger::html_file</pre>:

<pre>
logger::html_file html_logfile("log.html");
</pre>

The constructor of this file launches
<pre>logger::open_html_file("log.html")</pre>, and its destructor calls
<pre>logger::close_html_file();</pre> automatically (whether due to its
scope expiring, or an exception being thrown, etc)
</p>

<p><div class=sectiontitle><a name="output">Outputting text</a></div>

<p>
Whatever data (text, double, etc) can be written to the log using the
&lt;&lt; operator. It is a template function and can be called for any
type for which the same operator for std::ostream is implemented.
</p>

